@naveedahmed1 thnx for sharing the updated reproduction! Given the updated code I could see the following:
* the fix from https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/52742 is making massive difference so the actual fix from there works;
* I've understood the source of the slowness for the dropdown opening and and it is _not_ related to the new control flow in any way.

The dropdown menu opening is slow since clicking ion the toggle button runs change detection multiple times. In fact, the change detection is run as many times as there are rows in the jobs collection (!!!).

This is due to the `@HostListener('document:click', ['$event', '$event.target'])` in the `MenuDirective`. Each directive instance (present on each and every row): 
* will add a new event listener;
* all those event listeners will fire on each menu toggle;
* each event handler will trigger a _separate_ change detection.

There are many ways of addressing this issue, I would argue that the easiest one is to make sure that only one event listener is registered on the document level (this would require re-design of the menu directive). Another option is to use event coalescing on the zone.js level.

In any case, I believe that this is _not_ related to the new control flow - I think that you would see the same issue with `ngFor`. But I'm happy to look into it more if you can share a branch with the `ngFor`-based implementation and much better performance.
